(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-blood-disorders-quiz"]={"title":"Blood Disorders Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In acute leukemia, acquired mutations in which genes block the differentiation of immature cells?","choices":["Immunoglobulin genes","Coagulation factor genes","Red-cell membrane genes","Transcription factor genes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acquired mutations in transcription factors, which regulate the differentiation of immature lymphoid or myeloid cells, produce the maturation arrest of acute leukemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Into which cells do activated, selected B cells differentiate?","choices":["T cells or natural killer cells","Plasma cells or memory B cells","Lymphoblasts or myeloblasts","Monocytes or macrophages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After antigen activation and selection, B cells differentiate into plasma cells (which produce antibody) or memory B cells. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 24"},{"q":"What does von Willebrand factor do?","choices":["Activates factor X together with factor IXa","Degrades fibrin once a clot has formed","Supports platelet adhesion and stabilizes factor VIII","Limits coagulation together with protein S"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Von Willebrand factor supports platelet adhesion and carries and stabilizes factor VIII, which is why von Willebrand disease is listed with the hemophilias as a bleeding disorder. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which consequences do monoclonal proteins and marrow infiltration cause in myeloma?","choices":["Leukostasis in the lungs and brain","Thrombosis with a falling platelet count","Renal injury, anemia and impaired immunity","Raised hematocrit from plasma loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In multiple myeloma, monoclonal proteins and marrow infiltration can cause renal injury, anemia and impaired immunity; altered bone remodeling adds osteolytic lesions and hypercalcemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Microcytic hypochromic anemias are caused by disorders of what?","choices":["Hemoglobin synthesis","Erythroid precursor maturation","Red-cell membrane structure","Red-cell survival in the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Microcytic hypochromic anemias are caused by disorders of hemoglobin synthesis, which is why iron deficiency, thalassemia and sideroblastic anemia sit in the microcytic group. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"What does the PT (prothrombin time) assess?","choices":["Intrinsic factors XII, XI, IX and VIII","Extrinsic factor VII and the common factors","Plasmin's breakdown of fibrin","Platelet adhesion via von Willebrand factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The PT (prothrombin time) assesses the extrinsic factor (VII) and the common factors (X, V, II and I); the aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) assesses the intrinsic factors XII, XI, IX and VIII plus the common ones. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which appearance identifies a band neutrophil on a smear?","choices":["Two-lobed nucleus with large red secondary granules","Round nucleus with scant pale cytoplasm, no granules","Two to three lobes hidden by dark granules","Kidney-bean nucleus indented over half its width"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A band neutrophil has a kidney-bean nucleus with mature chromatin and no nucleoli, an indentation greater than half the diameter, and secondary granules only. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 13, 14"},{"q":"Why must both the relative and the absolute white cell counts be read?","choices":["A fall in one type can falsely raise another's percentage","The relative count gives the number of cells per microliter","Each cell's percentage is measured apart from the others","The absolute count ignores the total white blood cell count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Percentages are shares of one total, so an absolute decrease in one cell type can show an artificially increased relative count in another. Reading the absolute count shows whether that cell really rose. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is a malignancy of which cells?","choices":["Mature B cells","Germinal-center B cells","Differentiated plasma cells","Precursor B or T cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma is a malignancy of precursor B or T cells (lymphoblasts): B-ALL/LBL from the B-cell precursor in the marrow, T-ALL/LBL from the T-cell precursor in the thymus. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 23, 28"},{"q":"Which anemia is listed in the microcytic group?","choices":["Megaloblastic anemia","Sideroblastic anemia","Anemia of marrow failure","Anemia of renal disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The microcytic group (mean cell volume below 80 fL, femtoliters) lists iron deficiency, thalassemia, inflammation in some cases, and sideroblastic anemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the primary pathogenic defect in acute leukemia?","choices":["Maturation arrest","Ineffective hematopoiesis","Premature red-cell destruction","Reactive bone marrow stimulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The primary pathogenic defect in acute leukemia is maturation arrest: acquired mutations in transcription factors block differentiation, and other mutations let the cells proliferate independently of growth factors. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is relative erythrocytosis?","choices":["An increase in red-cell mass","A clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm","Less plasma without more red-cell mass","A marrow response to hypoxia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Relative erythrocytosis is reduced plasma volume without increased red-cell mass, such as dehydration; hemoglobin and hematocrit rise only because the plasma has shrunk. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Under the World Health Organization 2024 cutoffs (ages 15 to 65), below what hemoglobin is a nonpregnant woman anemic?","choices":["10 g/dL","11 g/dL","12 g/dL","13 g/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The World Health Organization 2024 cutoffs for ages 15 to 65 are a hemoglobin below 13 g/dL in men and below 12 g/dL in nonpregnant women. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which three causes should a raised eosinophil count bring to mind first?","choices":["Chronic infections such as tuberculosis","Allergy, parasites and drug effects","Acute bacterial inflammation and sepsis","Marrow malignancy before anything else"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Think of three things with increased eosinophils: allergy (especially asthma), a parasite, or a drug effect. Eosinophilia can rarely be neoplastic or autoimmune. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"In the healthy adult, where does almost all hematopoiesis occur?","choices":["Distal radius, tibia and the small hand bones","The thymus and the peripheral lymph nodes","The spleen and the liver sinusoids","Axial skeleton, proximal humerus and femur"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most hematopoiesis occurs in the bone marrow, and in the healthy adult almost all of it is in the axial skeleton plus the proximal humerus and femur. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia?","choices":["Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency","Iron deficiency","Liver disease or alcohol","Thalassemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the macrocytic group (mean cell volume above 100 fL, femtoliters), the megaloblastic causes are vitamin B12 or folate deficiency; liver disease, alcohol, drugs and reticulocytosis are the nonmegaloblastic causes. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does plasmin do?","choices":["Activates factor X","Degrades fibrin","Crosslinks fibrin","Converts fibrinogen to fibrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In fibrinolysis, plasminogen becomes plasmin, which degrades fibrin; this is separate from the regulation of coagulation by antithrombin and activated protein C. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"How is an absolute neutrophil count of 750 cells/µL graded?","choices":["Agranulocytosis","Severe neutropenia","Moderate neutropenia","Mild neutropenia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An absolute neutrophil count of 750 cells/µL falls in the moderate band, 500 to 1,000 cells/µL; severe is below 500 and mild is 1,000 to 1,500. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In V(D)J recombination, which gene segments do light chains use?","choices":["V and J","V, D and J","V and D","D and J"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Developing B cells rearrange their immunoglobulin genes in the marrow: heavy chains use V, D and J segments, while light chains use only V and J. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is bandemia?","choices":["More mature neutrophils in the blood","Fewer than 200 neutrophils/µL","More eosinophils from a drug effect","More immature neutrophils in the blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bandemia is an increase in immature (band) neutrophils, a left shift, which may accompany infection or other inflammatory stress. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two features classify the lymphoid neoplasms?","choices":["Blood count and marrow cellularity","Clonality and the white count","Patient age and cell size","Cell lineage and stage of maturation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoid neoplasms are classified by cell lineage and stage of maturation: precursor B or T cells, mature B cells, mature T and natural killer cells, and germinal-center B cells for classic Hodgkin lymphoma. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"After initiation, which enzyme-cofactor pair activates factor X?","choices":["Factor Xa with factor Va","Thrombin with factor XIIIa","Factor IXa with factor VIIIa","Plasmin with protein S"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the factor X activation step, IXa with its cofactor VIIIa activates X to Xa; Xa with Va then generates thrombin. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"Which disorder is one of the myeloproliferative neoplasms?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Hereditary spherocytosis","Essential thrombocythemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The myeloproliferative neoplasms are chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis: clonal proliferations of myeloid cells. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the cause of factor V Leiden thrombophilia?","choices":["A genetic mutation","An autoimmune response","A factor V deficiency","An immune reaction to heparin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is a thrombophilia (excessive clotting) caused by a genetic mutation; antiphospholipid syndrome, the other thrombophilia listed, is an autoimmune response. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In hereditary spherocytosis, why are the red cells destroyed early?","choices":["Antibodies lyse the cells within the circulation","Their hemoglobin synthesis fails in the marrow","Rigid spherocytes are trapped and eaten in the spleen","The marrow releases them before they mature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biconcave young cells lose lipid membrane on repeated passage through the spleen and circulation, become rigid, inflexible spherocytes, are trapped in the spleen and destroyed prematurely by splenic macrophages: extravascular hemolysis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the usual red-cell lifespan that hemolysis shortens?","choices":["About 30 days","About 60 days","About 120 days","About 180 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In hemolytic anemia red-cell survival is shortened below the usual lifespan of about 120 days, hemoglobin breakdown products increase, and a compensating marrow raises reticulocyte production. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which process defines the myeloproliferative neoplasms?","choices":["Clonal proliferation of myeloid cells","Ineffective hematopoiesis with dysplasia","Impaired differentiation with blast growth","Clonal growth of antibody-making B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myeloproliferative neoplasms are a clonal proliferation of myeloid cells: chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does hypochromia of red cells reflect?","choices":["Reduced cell size","Reduced hemoglobinization","Abnormal cell shape","Increased cell volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The degree of hemoglobinization is reflected in the color of red cells: normochromic or hypochromic. Hypochromic cells carry less hemoglobin, as in the microcytic hypochromic anemias of impaired hemoglobin synthesis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 32"},{"q":"Which anemia results from diminished erythropoiesis?","choices":["Aplastic anemia","Hereditary spherocytosis","Anemia of acute blood loss","Extravascular hemolytic anemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The anemias of diminished erythropoiesis are megaloblastic anemia, vitamin B12 deficiency anemia, iron deficiency anemia and aplastic anemia: too little production rather than loss or destruction. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which progenitor gives rise to megakaryocytes?","choices":["Lymphoid progenitor","Myeloid progenitor","Erythroid precursor","Granulocyte precursor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the simplified lineage model the multipotent stem cell gives myeloid and lymphoid progenitors, and the myeloid progenitor gives erythroid precursors, megakaryocytes (which make platelets), granulocytes and monocytes. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
